  "answer_text": "The iconic scene where Del Boy falls through the bar is from the episode **\"Yuppy Love\"**! Here are all the details:\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udcfa The Episode: *\"Yuppy Love\"*\n**\"Yuppy Love\"** was the first episode of **Series 6** of *Only Fools and Horses*, airing on **8th January 1989** on BBC1, drawing a huge viewing figure of **13.9 million**.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83c\udf78 The Scene\nIn a wine bar, Del is leaning against a folding section at the end of the bar when he sees an attractive young woman looking at him. A barman comes along and lifts up the section Del was leaning against to get through, but doesn't put it back down, leaving a gap \u2014 all behind Del's back without him noticing. Del then says *\"Play it nice and cool, son. Nice and cool, you know what I mean...\"* \u2014 and leans back, falling straight through the gap onto the floor.\n\nJust as funny as the fall itself was **Trigger's baffled reaction** to Del's sudden disappearance.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83c\udfad Behind the Scenes\n- The scene was inspired by writer **John Sullivan** watching a real man do the exact same thing in a wine bar, except the real man grabbed onto the fixed part of the bar so he didn't fall all the way over. Sullivan found the man's body language, trying to recover his cool, hilarious.\n\n- Sullivan originally wanted a slip, stumble, and tree-like fall, but **David Jason** thought Del should go all the way over \u2014 starting to go sideways and going over without looking in the direction of the fall, which Jason felt was the key to the scene. There was a hidden crash mat, but it was a hard shot to get right because it was difficult not to look where he was falling.\n\n- A rehearsal script that went up for auction revealed that **Trigger wasn't supposed to be in the scene originally**. However, Roger Lloyd Pack happened to be there on the day, and John Sullivan decided he should be included after all.\n\n- Roger Lloyd Pack later revealed that Jason never did the fall in rehearsal, so his shocked reaction was partly genuine: *\"He never did it in rehearsal, so it was a surprise when it came.\"*\n\n---\n\nDel Boy's fall through the bar has become **one of the most celebrated sitcom scenes ever**. David Jason says he gets people asking him about that fall all the time, and is happy to be remembered for something so iconic.",
